The conformance set of the resolution of a module manifest (seam 3)
====================================================================

Each directory here is one case. The function test_manifest_conformance in
tests/run.sh runs each case through mcp_load (lib/mcp.sh) and compares the
result with the expected file. A later Go core must give the same results.

This file gives version 1 of the dump format.


The files of a case
-------------------

module.toml    The manifest.
context        Lines "<key>=<value>":
                 name=<the module name>
                 origin=shared | local
               "shared" puts the module into mcp/, "local" into mcp.local/.
settings.toml  Optional. The setting values, as config.toml holds them.
               The runner reads the keys of the [settings] of the manifest
               from this file.
expected       The dump, when the status is 0.
expected.err   The status and the error, when the status is not 0.

The existence of a Podman secret is not an input: the start checks it,
not the resolution.


The dump (expected)
-------------------

- One line "<key>=<value>" for each item. The file is UTF-8, with a
  newline after each line.
- A value has these escapes, in the key too: "\" is "\\", a newline is
  "\n", and "=" is "\=". These three are enough: the reader refuses each
  other control character, and allows a newline only in a help text.
- An empty value gives no line. Exception: each setting of the manifest
  has its "settings.<KEY>" line, also with an empty value.
- The lines are sorted by bytes (LC_ALL=C sort), after the escapes.
- A list gives one line for each item, with the index from 0. The items
  keep the order of the manifest.

The keys:

description                  The description of the module.
settings.<KEY>               The value after the default. An empty value
                             counts as not set, and gets the default.
settings.<KEY>.required      1 for a required setting.
settings.<KEY>.help          The help of the setting.
field.<KEY>.<field>          A field of a check (MCP_FIELD). Ticket 06
                             fills it; empty until then.
items.<KEY>                  The checked items of a list setting
                             (MCP_ITEMS). Ticket 06 fills it; empty until
                             then.
servers.<n>.name             The name of a server.
servers.<n>.url              Its URL.
servers.<n>.caller           1 when the server gets the caller token.
servers.<n>.description      Its description: the description of the
                             module when the server has none.
secrets.<n>                  The Podman name of a secret that must exist
                             before the start: "<module>_<local name>".
                             A secret with for_each has no line; ticket 06
                             adds its names.
secret_help.<Podman name>    The help of that secret.
secret.<local name>          The Podman name for each local name: the
                             secrets, the own secrets and mcp_caller
                             (MCP_SECRET).
own_secrets.<n>              The Podman name of a secret that the code of
                             the module makes.
caller_secret                The Podman name of the caller token,
                             "<module>_mcp_caller", when a server has
                             caller = true.
image.<local name>           The image reference.
egress.<n>                   A host that the agent can reach.


The error (expected.err)
------------------------

The first line is "status=<n>", the status of mcp_load:

1   A setting is missing or fails a check. The module stays out of the
    start, and agentbox removes its access.
2   The module is broken.

Then one line for each problem:

- Status 1: "<KEY>: <code>", one line for each setting, in the order of
  the manifest.
- Status 2: "module.toml:<line>: <code>" for the first error only. The
  line is the line of the key, or of its table header. A key with no
  line gives "module.toml: <code>": a key in an inline table, a table
  with no keys that is not the first table of its kind, and the error
  reserved-name.

The first error: the errors of the reader come first, in the order of
the reader. Then the manifest unit checks in this order, and stops at the
first error: bad-format, reserved-name, reserved-secret, no-server, then
each server in its order (bad-server-name, duplicate-server,
pipe-in-server). A missing setting (status 1) comes last.


The error codes
---------------

Status 1:

missing-setting     A required setting has no value and no default.

Status 2, from the manifest unit (lib/mcp.sh):

bad-format          "format" is 0 or lower. The first format is 1.
reserved-name       The module has the name "git". The prefix "git_" of
                    the secret names is for the modules from git. The
                    name is not a key, so the error has no line.
reserved-secret     The manifest has [secrets.mcp_caller]. agentbox makes
                    this secret for a server with caller = true. The line
                    of the table header.
no-server           The manifest has no [[servers]]. The line of
                    "format".
bad-server-name     A server name is not lowercase letters, digits and
                    "-", with a letter or a digit first. A "|" in a
                    server name gives this code.
duplicate-server    Two servers of the manifest have the same name. The
                    line of the second [[servers]].
pipe-in-server      The URL or the description of a server has a "|".
                    The description can come from the description of the
                    module, for a server with no description; then the
                    line is the line of "description".

Status 2, from the TOML reader (lib/toml-reader.py; the reader set in
tests/toml/manifest/ has the cases):

syntax, newer-format, multi-line-string, unknown-key, dot-in-key,
quoted-key, bad-name, type, table, missing-key, float, date,
control-character
